.TH FLEET-SANITIZE 1 "2026-11-10" "cti-agent 1.6" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
fleet-sanitize \- clean text before the orchestrator's model reads it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I command
|
.B fleet\-sanitize
.RB [ \-\-source
.IR NAME ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fleet\-sanitize
reads standard input and writes it to standard output with the characters a
model should not be handed removed. Most of what the orchestrator heartbeat
reads (the journal, the board, the memory database) began in somebody else's
mail or feed, and hidden characters are a way to smuggle instructions past a
human reader.
.PP
It removes control characters other than newline and tab, C1 controls,
bidirectional overrides and zero\-width characters. A line over 1024
characters is cut and ends with a
.B [cut N chars]
marker. Output over 64 KB is cut, with a note. Every line it changed is
logged to the journal under the tag
.BR cti\-sanitize ;
it never writes to standard error, because standard error goes back into the
model.
.PP
The wrappers
.BR fleet\-journal (1),
.BR fleet\-board (1)
(read and tail),
.BR fleet\-db (1),
.BR cti\-kev (1)
and
.BR cti\-mailbox (1)
call it only inside a heartbeat, that is, when
.B CHECKIN_BEAT_ID
is set by
.BR run\-checkin (1).
Outside a heartbeat their output is untouched, so a lane that parses it is
unaffected. If the cleaner cannot run, the wrapper prints nothing: raw output
is not an option inside a beat. The Go twin is
.IR internal/sanitize ;
both read the same cases from
.IR internal/sanitize/testdata/sanitize\-cases.json .
.PP
PrimeSec design review 39260, Scenario\-222. The Read grant on
.IR reports/ ,
.I logs/
and
.I archive/
does not pass through the wrappers, so file reads are not cleaned; that gap
is accepted (see SECURITY.md).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-source " NAME"
A label for the journal lines (letters, digits,
.BR \- ,
.B _
and
.BR . ,
at most 40 characters). Default:
.BR unknown .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CHECKIN_BEAT_ID
Not read here. The wrappers use it to decide whether to call this tool.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/cti\-agent/bin/fleet\-sanitize
The cleaner, root\-owned.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The text was written.
.TP
.B 2
An argument other than
.B \-\-source
was given.
.SH EXAMPLES
Clean a file by hand and see what the journal recorded:
.PP
.EX
fleet\-sanitize \-\-source test < advisory.txt
sudo journalctl \-t cti\-sanitize \-\-since today
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run\-checkin (1),
.BR checkin\-bash\-guard (1),
.BR fleet\-journal (1),
.BR fleet\-board (1),
.BR fleet\-db (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
